.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt IOMS 4D
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ioms
.Nd AMD Zen IOMS nexus driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /devices/df@*/ioms@*
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver represents the I/O master/slave
.Pq IOMS
units found in the data fabric of the AMD Zen family processors used by the
Oxide architecture.
Each device node is a child of the
.Xr df 4D
node representing the data fabric instance the unit is attached to, and its
unit address is the die-relative IOMS index.
Each IOMS connects the data fabric to an I/O hub
.Pq IOHUB
containing an IOHUB core
.Pq IOHC ,
which hosts one PCIe root complex along with per-unit resources such as an
IOMMU and IOAPIC.
One instance of
.Nm
exists for every IOMS in the system.
.Pp
Each device node carries properties identifying the underlying hardware:
the data fabric node ID of the containing I/O die
.Pq Sy node-id ,
the die-relative IOMS and NBIO indices
.Pq Sy ioms , Sy nbio ,
the register instance identity of the I/O hub
.Pq Sy iohub , Sy iohc , Sy iohc-type ,
the bus number of the root bus hosted by the unit's PCIe root complex
.Pq Sy pci-bus ,
and the fabric identifier to which transactions targeting the unit are
routed
.Pq Sy fabric-id .
.Pp
At present these nodes serve to make the I/O fabric topology visible in the
device tree; the PCIe root complexes and other subordinate units remain
enumerated separately at the root of the tree.
Over time this driver is expected to become the parent of the corresponding
root complex and FCH nodes and to take ownership of per-unit resource
management.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr df 4D ,
.Xr npe 4D
